  "conclusion": "SODIUM: at 1.5x, 2x and 4x both models move materially and in OPPOSITE directions -- the differentiable model fires 21 to 53 % more while NEURON fires 7 to 36 % less, because NEURON enters depolarization block. At 0.5x and 0.75x the differentiable model collapses (to 3 % and 62 % of wild type) while NEURON barely moves. POTASSIUM: at all 6 factors both move materially and in OPPOSITE directions -- scaling potassium up silences the differentiable model (2 % at 1.5x, 0 % at 4x) and slightly increases NEURON's firing (106 % at 1.5x, 112 % at 4x). LEAK: the differentiable model is essentially insensitive (97 to 100 % of wild type across the whole 0.25x to 4x range) while NEURON swings from 158 % to 0 %. So the naive multiplicative bridge does not carry information: a candidate the differentiable model ranks highly is not one the NEURON model would rank highly.",
